<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Package xmlns="http://soap.sforce.com/2006/04/metadata">
    <!--
    Phase B4 of the "Property Package -> Portfolio Deal" rename program.

    STATUS: PREPARED, NOT AUTHORISED. Do not deploy without the user's explicit
    go-ahead. This file only DESCRIBES a deletion; it performs nothing on its own.

    An Apex class rename and an LWC bundle rename are both a delete-and-create at
    the Metadata API level -- there is no "rename" operation. Phase B4 created the
    6 new classes and 2 new LWC bundles listed in this manifest's companion
    package.xml under their new names (PropertyPackage* -> PortfolioDeal*,
    recentPackages -> recentPortfolioDeals, packageSiblings -> portfolioDealSiblings)
    and repointed every caller to the new names. This file deletes the 6 old
    classes and 2 old LWC bundles left behind by that rename.

    POST-destructive (not pre-): the FlexiPages (Lead_Funnel, Lead_Record_Page,
    Opportunity_Record_Page) and DPEG_Apex_Access's classAccesses already stop
    referencing the old names in the companion package.xml, so by the time this
    file's deletions run, nothing in the constructive payload still points at the
    components being deleted. Deleting PRE would risk a dangling reference window
    mid-transaction if ordering assumptions ever changed; POST leaves no such
    window because the repoint has already landed.

    Without this manifest, this org (usman-dpeg, live, no source tracking) has no
    mechanism to remove the old components: a plain `--source-dir` deploy only
    adds/updates what's present locally, it never deletes what's absent. Left
    undeleted, PropertyPackageController/Selector/Service (+ their 3 test classes)
    and the recentPackages/packageSiblings LWC bundles would stay live and
    orphaned in the org -- their permission grant already moved to
    PortfolioDealController in this same wave (DPEG_Apex_Access), so the old
    classes would be unreachable dead code and the old LWC bundles would be
    unreferenced by any FlexiPage, while still consuming class/bundle name space
    and still showing up in metadata retrieves and coverage reports.

    DELETES EXACTLY 6 APEX CLASSES AND 2 LWC BUNDLES:
      ApexClass:
        - PropertyPackageController      -> PortfolioDealController
        - PropertyPackageControllerTest  -> PortfolioDealControllerTest
        - PropertyPackageSelector        -> PortfolioDealSelector
        - PropertyPackageSelectorTest    -> PortfolioDealSelectorTest
        - PropertyPackageService         -> PortfolioDealService
        - PropertyPackageServiceTest     -> PortfolioDealServiceTest
      LightningComponentBundle:
        - recentPackages    -> recentPortfolioDeals
        - packageSiblings   -> portfolioDealSiblings

    NOTE ON PropertyPackageController/ControllerTest: these two class names also
    appear as members of manifest/portfolio-deal-a3/package.xml (deploy
    0Afiw000000LgNdCAK, executed 2026-08-17), where they received a COMMENT-ONLY
    rewrite alongside that phase's Opportunity field deletion. That earlier wave
    did not rename or delete them -- it left them live under their original names.
    This phase (B4) is the one that actually renames them; A3's already-executed
    manifest is being annotated (not modified in substance) to record that 4 of
    its 6 members no longer exist under these names as of this deploy. See
    manifest/portfolio-deal-a3/package.xml's updated STATUS line.

    NOT included here, and deliberately so: Property_Package__c (the object),
    its fields, and the Opportunity/Lead/Property__c Property_Package__c lookup
    fields all stay live. Phase B4 is Apex/LWC only. Object and field retirement
    is Phase B5's scope, not this one's -- see the ADDITIVE ONLY comments on the
    Portfolio_Deal__c grants added to the permission sets in this same wave's
    package.xml, which explicitly defer that retirement.

    Deploy (only once authorised by the user; devops runs a validation-only
    dry-run first per standing project practice):
      sf project deploy start \
        --manifest manifest/portfolio-deal-b4/package.xml \
        --post-destructive-changes manifest/portfolio-deal-b4/destructiveChangesPost.xml \
        --target-org usman-dpeg \
        --test-level RunLocalTests --wait 30

    Do NOT pass --purge-on-delete -- documented as non-functional on production
    orgs and fails silently rather than erroring (see manifest/portfolio-deal-a3).

    Rollback: deleted Apex classes and LWC bundles are recoverable from git
    history (this same commit removes them from force-app/main/default), but are
    NOT recoverable from any org-side recycle bin the way custom fields are --
    Apex/LWC deletions are not soft-deleted by the platform. Re-deploying the old
    source and reverting DPEG_Apex_Access's classAccesses entry would restore
    function, but any org data or usage that accrued against the NEW class Ids in
    the interim would not follow.
    -->
    <types>
        <members>PropertyPackageController</members>
        <members>PropertyPackageControllerTest</members>
        <members>PropertyPackageSelector</members>
        <members>PropertyPackageSelectorTest</members>
        <members>PropertyPackageService</members>
        <members>PropertyPackageServiceTest</members>
        <name>ApexClass</name>
    </types>
    <types>
        <members>recentPackages</members>
        <members>packageSiblings</members>
        <name>LightningComponentBundle</name>
    </types>
    <version>67.0</version>
</Package>
